      "hints": [
        "Expand LHS and equate to RHS: $\\sin^2\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta - 2\\cos^2\\theta = 0$.",
        "Replace $\\sin^2\\theta = 1 - \\cos^2\\theta$ to show $3\\cos^2\\theta - 2\\cos\\theta - 1 = 0$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Expand the left-hand side of the trigonometric equation:\n$$\\text{LHS} = (\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta)(1 + \\sin\\theta - \\cos\\theta)$$\n$$= \\sin\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta - \\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta + 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta - 2\\cos^2\\theta$$\n$$= \\sin\\theta + \\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta - 2\\cos^2\\theta$$",
        "Expand the right-hand side:\n$$\\text{RHS} = \\sin\\theta(1 + \\cos\\theta) = \\sin\\theta + \\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$$",
        "Equate $\\text{LHS} = \\text{RHS}$ and cancel common terms:\n$$\\sin^2\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta - 2\\cos^2\\theta = 0$$",
        "Substitute the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2\\theta = 1 - \\cos^2\\theta$:\n$$(1 - \\cos^2\\theta) + 2\\cos\\theta - 2\\cos^2\\theta = 0 \\implies 1 + 2\\cos\\theta - 3\\cos^2\\theta = 0$$\n$$3\\cos^2\\theta - 2\\cos\\theta - 1 = 0$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Trigonometric Proof": "Expands both sides to $\\sin^2\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta - 2\\cos^2\\theta = 0$ (B1). Applies $\\sin^2\\theta = 1 - \\cos^2\\theta$ (M1). Obtains $3\\cos^2\\theta - 2\\cos\\theta - 1 = 0$ (A1)."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
      }
